2025-11-27 13:59:25,380 - app.llm.services - DEBUG - [REQUESTS] Request body: b'{"model": "llama3.2:latest", "prompt": "You are analyzing news articles for a Scottish heritage and culture newsletter with a primarily US-Scots diaspora audience - people of Scottish descent living in the United States, Canada, Australia, and other countries who maintain an interest in the history and culture of their \\"old country\\".\\n\\nImagine a typical reader: someone of Scottish descent living in the US, with family connections to Scotland, interested in learning about the \\"old country\\" - its history, culture, traditions, and contemporary developments that matter.\\n\\nArticle Title: UK Budget Reaction: Reeves leaves business rates dilemma for Scottish Government\\nURL: https://www.scotsman.com/business/uk-budget-reaction-reeves-leaves-business-rates-dilemma-for-scottish-government-5420126\\n\\nArticle Content (first 2000 chars):\\nAfter weeks of briefing, revised briefing, and endless speculation the preamble to this year\\u2019sUK Budgetwas somewhat surreal. Watching Treasury Ministers pass phones to the Chancellor as they tried to come to terms with the leak of the OBR\\u2019s forecasts felt like a scene from TV\\u2019s The Thick of It. That somewhat took away the element of surprise from much of the Chancellor\\u2019s statement. Mind you, even without the leak, it would have been hard to generate much excitement at 1.5 per cent GDP growth for the foreseeable future. Retailers will be disappointed, but not surprised to learn consumption growth is to average around one percent in 2025 and 2026. That tracks the 0.7 per cent average growth for Scottish retail sales over the last 12 months; so few signs trading will improve much in the next two years. Advertisement Advertisement Beyond the economic update the Chancellor had a blizzard of announcements. For retailers there was an expected rise in the National Living Wage. Changes to salary sacrifice pension arrangements which will hurt retail employees and businesses alike. On the positive side of the ledger the removal of VAT on direct charitable donations will better help retailers support good causes. Closing the de minimis loophole on low-value imports is welcome \\u2013 albeit it\\u2019s ridiculous to delay that until 2029. From a parochial perspective Scottish Ministers will receive \\u00a3820 million throughBarnett Consequentialrevenues. However, for retailers the key announcement was always going to be the changes for business rates in England. The Chancellor announced most retail and hospitality businesses would see a permanent reduction in their non-domestic rates bills; paid for by a surtax on the very largest properties. Whilst in the Commons she implied this covered large online warehouses, the truth is every shop with a rateable value over \\u00a3500,000 would see a business rates rise. As we saw last year with last year\\u2019s EmployerNational Insurance rises, these tax increases en\\n\\nYour task:\\n1. Rate this article on FIVE separate dimensions, each on a scale of 1-100. CRITICAL: Most stories should have MOST dimensions scoring 10-40. Only truly exceptional stories should have dimensions above 70. Be CRITICAL and use LOW scores liberally.\\n\\n   HISTORICAL INTEREST (1-100): \\n   - Scores 1-20: No historical content, just current events\\n   - Scores 21-40: Minor historical mention or local history\\n   - Scores 41-60: Some historical context\\n   - Scores 61-80: Substantial historical content, heritage sites, notable historical figures\\n   - Scores 81-100: Major historical discoveries, archaeology, preservation of national heritage\\n\\n   CULTURAL INTEREST (1-100):\\n   - Scores 1-20: No cultural content, just daily news\\n   - Scores 21-40: Minor cultural mention\\n   - Scores 41-60: Some cultural context\\n   - Scores 61-80: Substantial cultural content (traditions, language, arts)\\n   - Scores 81-100: Major cultural significance, festivals, cultural revivals\