2025-11-27 14:00:50,162 - app.llm.services - DEBUG - [REQUESTS] Request body: b'{"model": "llama3.2:latest", "prompt": "You are analyzing news articles for a Scottish heritage and culture newsletter with a primarily US-Scots diaspora audience - people of Scottish descent living in the United States, Canada, Australia, and other countries who maintain an interest in the history and culture of their \\"old country\\".\\n\\nImagine a typical reader: someone of Scottish descent living in the US, with family connections to Scotland, interested in learning about the \\"old country\\" - its history, culture, traditions, and contemporary developments that matter.\\n\\nArticle Title: Glasgow City Council is undercharging GlasGLOW for use of park, claim residents\\nURL: https://www.heraldscotland.com/news/25646924.volunteers-raise-alarm-glasglows-impact-council-fees/?ref=rss\\n\\nArticle Content (first 2000 chars):\\nBotanic Gardens GlasgowGlasgow City CouncilWest EndCultural attractionsEntertainmentEventsLeisureLocal governmentGlasgow Glasgow volunteers and residents have raised concerns over whether the fees the council is charging for Itison\\u2019s GlasGLOW adequately reflect its impact on a public green space. Each year since 2018, the Botanic Gardens have been transformed into a private sound and light show that attracts tens of thousands of people to the West End over the Halloween period. But community members have expressed concern over whether the event is being charged appropriately for use of the park byGlasgow City Counciland whether the local authority\\u2019s Environmental Levy is fit for purpose. An invoice seen exclusively by The Herald reveals that the Council charged itison an Environmental Levy of \\u00a311,475 (based on a calculation of 50p x 1350 capacity x 17 days) for use of the Botanics for GlasGLOW in 2023. The event company was charged \\u00a31607 per day for the hire of the Botanic Gardens for the 17 days that GlasGLOW was operational. Hire fees are not charged for set-up or teardown of events. The Botanic Gardens in the West End of Glasgow(Image: Colin Mearns) GlasGLOW at the Botanics.(Image: Newsquest Archive) The figures were revealed to the public as part of the Annual Accounts Inspection for 2023/24. A spokesperson for the council refused multiple requests to comment on the figures. They said: \\u201cWe do not discuss commercially sensitive information.\\u201d The Ghostbusters-inspired event was expected to attract 90,000 visitors to the Botanic Gardens in 2023, operating from October 27 to November 5. Ticket prices started at \\u00a324 for adults and \\u00a314 for children, with \\"mallow\\" fast passes costing \\u00a332 for adults and \\u00a322 for children. The council says the levy is based on capacity rather than tickets sold, applying to the maximum number of people in the park at one time, not the total number of tickets sold across multiple sessions. The council does not double the capacity figure for\\n\\nYour task:\\n1. Rate this article on FIVE separate dimensions, each on a scale of 1-100. CRITICAL: Most stories should have MOST dimensions scoring 10-40. Only truly exceptional stories should have dimensions above 70. Be CRITICAL and use LOW scores liberally.\\n\\n   HISTORICAL INTEREST (1-100): \\n   - Scores 1-20: No historical content, just current events\\n   - Scores 21-40: Minor historical mention or local history\\n   - Scores 41-60: Some historical context\\n   - Scores 61-80: Substantial historical content, heritage sites, notable historical figures\\n   - Scores 81-100: Major historical discoveries, archaeology, preservation of national heritage\\n\\n   CULTURAL INTEREST (1-100):\\n   - Scores 1-20: No cultural content, just daily news\\n   - Scores 21-40: Minor cultural mention\\n   - Scores 41-60: Some cultural context\\n   - Scores 61-80: Substantial cultural content (traditions, language, arts)\\n   - Scores 81-100: Major cultural significance, festivals, cultural revivals\\n\\n 